      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"thermal-relationships\">Thermal relationships</h1>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Focus variables in this notebook: <code>temperature_2m</code>, <code>dew_point_2m</code>, <code>apparent_temperature</code>, <code>soil_temperature_0_to_7cm</code>.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Pick one focus variable and Spark will compare it with the <strong>other 16 selected\nweather variables</strong>. The first view ranks Pearson\ncorrelations; the second lets you inspect any pair as a sampled scatterplot.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Correlation is descriptive, not causal. Weather variables are autocorrelated,\nseasonal, and often derived from each other. <code>weather_code</code> is categorical,\nwind direction is circular, and <code>is_day</code> is binary, so interpret their Pearson\ncoefficients cautiously.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
